# Backend — architecture & reliability

Keep Nest as the delivery mechanism. Business rules must not be trapped in controllers or Prisma calls.

## Layering (pragmatic Clean Architecture)

| Layer | Responsibility |
|-------|----------------|
| **Controller** | HTTP mapping, auth guards, DTO/Zod pipe — no business branching |
| **Service / use-case** | Domain rules, orchestration, transactions |
| **Prisma / Storage** | Persistence & files only — swap-friendly via interfaces where it matters |

```ts
// ❌ BAD — controller writes Prisma + hashes password + sets cookies inline
// ✅ GOOD — controller → AuthService; storage via StorageService; cookies helper isolated
```

- Contracts live in `@nechto/api-contract`; API DTOs re-export, do not fork schemas
- Env via Zod (`config/env.ts`) — **fail-fast** on boot if invalid
- Validate input at the boundary; reject early (`400` / `401` / `403` / `404`)

## Idempotency & safety

- Safe methods (`GET`, `HEAD`) stay side-effect free
- Mutating retries must not double-create (unique constraints, idempotency keys where needed)
- Auth: JWT httpOnly cookies + `JwtAuthGuard`; never store plaintext passwords
- Uploads only through `StorageService` — no ad-hoc disk/S3 in feature modules

## Scalability (design for multi-instance)

- No required in-process-only state for correctness (sessions in DB/cookie JWT already)
- Avoid local-memory caches as source of truth
- Background work / locks later via DB or queue — not “global variable on this server”
- Stateless API containers behind Compose/prod reverse proxy

## Performance

- Prevent **N+1** — explicit `include`/`select`; index foreign keys and filter columns (`slug`, tags, `createdAt`)
- Return only needed fields; paginate list endpoints from day one when feeds appear
- Transactions for multi-step writes that must stay consistent
- Do not log PII/secrets; keep hot paths free of sync heavy CPU (hashing is fine; huge image work → async later)

## Errors

- Map domain failures to stable HTTP semantics
- No empty `catch`; no “return null” for auth failures — throw / reject clearly
